# Research Paper Image Paths

## Rule: always use absolute paths in Markdown

All `![caption](path)` references inside `docnav/Research/papers/*.md` must use
**absolute filesystem paths**, e.g.:

```markdown
![Caption.](/home/jw/src/tv/docnav/Research/papers/17_ostrom-tholonic-governance/figures/17_cpr-tholon-triangle.png)
```

**Why:** The `.md` file sits at `docnav/Research/papers/<N>_<slug>.md` while its
figures live one level deeper at `docnav/Research/papers/<N>_<slug>/figures/`.
A relative path like `17_ostrom-tholonic-governance/figures/foo.png` renders
correctly in the file-manager tree but breaks the IDE Markdown preview.  An
absolute path renders correctly everywhere.

## How the PDF build handles this

`scripts/pandoc_paper_postprocess.py` automatically rewrites every absolute image
path to a path relative to the output `.tex` directory before writing the final
`.tex`.  The rewrite rule is:

```
/home/jw/src/tv/docnav/Research/papers/<N>_<slug>/figures/foo.png
  →  figures/foo.png
```

`pdflatex` runs from inside `<N>_<slug>/` (see `scripts/research_paper_pdflatex.sh`),
so `figures/foo.png` resolves correctly at compile time.

## Figure storage convention

Paper-specific figures live in `docnav/Research/papers/<N>_<slug>/figures/`.
Shared figures (used across multiple papers) live in
`docnav/Research/papers/figures/` (the shared pool).

The pdflatex script creates a symlink `<paper-dir>/figures -> ../figures` only
when the paper dir has no `figures/` directory of its own and the `.tex` references
`{figures/...}`.  Paper-specific figures do not need this symlink.

## Naming convention

```
<N>_<short-descriptor>.png
```

Examples: `17_cpr-tholon-triangle.png`, `14_ndc-cancer-triangle.png`.

## Checklist

- [ ] Every `![...]()` in the `.md` uses an absolute path starting with `/home/jw/src/tv/`
- [ ] The figure file exists at that absolute path before running the PDF build
- [ ] The postprocessor is invoked with `--md <paper>.md` so path rewriting is active
